> calculate basic SNA indices on these networks. after we get descriptives 
> finished, we intend to do some ERGM related work. the data are keyed by 
> an ID number that refers to the particular network. each network is in 
> an edgelist. each line contains the network ID, the vertex ids for each 
> edge, some vertex attributes, and some edge attributes.

Just to clarify, I am assuming that your last two sentences override 
your first sentence: i.e., your data is not in sociomatrix (adjacency 
matrix) form.

>  
> the easy one first (and this absolutely displays my ignorance of R 
> operations)
> how, given that these data are read in as a flat matrix, do i point to 
> the edgelist portions of the data so that the network package can 
> populate the network correctly? basically, how do i say: these data 
> refer to this particular network, these columns represent the ids for 
> the edges, these columns represent vertex attributes for a particular 
> edge, etc. i need R to look at the first column to limit the edge 
> lists, and then look at the second and third columns for the vertices of 
> each edge.

This is a general R question, but you can probably do something like this:

#I assume that mydat is your data object, w/three columns, and all
#networks are of order n.  Also, I presume that your vertex IDs are
#integers from 1 to n; if not, this won't work as-is.  (I also assume
#that the data is directed.)
n<-12                            #Set the network size
netids<-sort(unique(mydat[,1]))  #Get the network IDs
mynets<-lapply(netids,function(id){
   network.edgelist(mydat[mydat[,1]==id,2:3],network.initialize(n))
})

I wrote this off the top of my head, so it may or may not work.  But a 
little uncertainty makes life more exciting.

>  
> the harder one is:
> i know carter's network object can handle all of these data for a single 
> network. (nodes, edges, node and edge attributes). but can the network 
> objects be grouped together so that i can have a data structure that 
> contains all 500 or so network objects and i can run the analyses on on 
> them at the same time?

Yes -- just put them in a list (per the above).  Nearly all sna routines 
accept networks/adjacency matrices/adjacency arrays/etc. in list form, 
and will generally treat the results in the obvious fashion.  For 
instance, gden(mynets) after executing the above should give you the 
vector of density scores for the 500 networks.

Hope that helps,

-Carter

