One extra comment on Dave's reply:

David Hunter wrote:
> Or perhaps I'd rather get, say, the Krackhardt connectedness score for 
> each of my 20 networks (using the sna function "connectedness"):
> 
>  > sapply(mynetworks, connectedness)
>  [1] 0.6 1.0 0.3 1.0 1.0 1.0 0.6 1.0 0.6 1.0 0.6 1.0 1.0 0.6 1.0 1.0 0.3 
> 1.0 0.3 1.0

Actually, you don't have to do this: "connectedness" (like most sna 
functions) is smart enough to recognize that it was called with a list, 
and to act accordingly.  Thus, you can get the same results with the 
command "connectedness(mynetworks)."  Either will work fine; the latter 
is just shorter.  (Note that there are some functions which require the 
sapply method -- centralization is a prominent one.  See the package 
help for details on how a given function processes its inputs.)

Hope that helps,

-Carter

PS. If your graphs are of the same order, you can also use adjacency 
arrays.  See ?sna for more.

